{"author":{"id":"b409816c5cd5ab140d1d52eb33aace35de44952bb794b19806fd57f6ecb1ace9"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1789229391,"nonce":"5HRbl+NpwGJNdgNE8KEZO7yMKX4=","title":"A value of type Any prints as its box address and cannot be returned from a branch","message":"def show(x: Any) { println(x) }\n    def pick(flag: bool): Any { if flag { 7 } else { \"fallback\" } }\n    def main() {\n        show(5)              // 47595619840\n        show(\"text\")         // 4423024652\n        let a: Any = 42\n        show(a)              // 47595619936\n        println(pick(true))  // 0\n    }\n\nEvery `show` prints an address: the argument arrives as a DynamicBox\npointer (boxed at the call site for the Any parameter), and `println`\nhands it to `$IO$println_dynamic`, whose registered dynamic signature\nmakes the backend box it a second time, as an integer. The runtime\nthen formats the outer box: an int holding the inner box's address.\n\n`pick` returns 0: an `if` expression whose declared type is Any has\nbranches of i64 and String that are never boxed to meet the declared\ntype, and the merge is read back as nothing.\n\nBoth matter for any frontend that leans on Any, which is where the\nPython frontend's dynamic values go. What is needed: a value whose HIR\ntype is already a box pointer must pass through the backend's\nsignature-driven boxing untouched, and an expression whose declared\ntype is Any boxes each branch value before the merge, the way a call\nargument or a return does.","files":null}]}